{"arrivo":"et","origine":"bat","origine_nome":"Baltic","n":10,"n_alternative":5,"parole":[{"parola":"agu","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unknown","significato":"morning twilight","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"bat","nome":"Baltic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gem-pro","nome":"Proto-Germanic","termine":"*hakkōną","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"gem-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Unknown. Several theories exist: * Of Baltic origin (compare Latvian sakas and Lithuanian šaka). * From Proto-Germanic *hakkōną. * From the same source as Lithuanian šokti. * From the same source as Finnish häkä."},{"parola":"allikas","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Possibly","significato":"spring (source of water)","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"bat","nome":"Baltic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"bat","ambiti":["arte"],"via":"","testo":"Cognate to Finnish allikko. Possibly from a Baltic language, compare Lithuanian šaltenis (“spring”) and šaltinis (“spring, source, that which is cold”). While the expected initial h- is seen in older Estonian texts, its loss in Finnish is unexplained, possibly due to similar words starting with ala-"},{"parola":"kaim","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"relative, tribesman, companion","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"bat","nome":"Baltic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"bat","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from a Baltic language, compare Lithuanian kaimas. and Latvian ciems. Cognate to Finnish kaima."},{"parola":"rändama","pos":"verb","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"to travel","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"bat","nome":"Baltic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"bat","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From a